WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:01.600
Picture this scenario for a second.

00:02.120 --> 00:06.980
You're observing a massive multibillion dollar corporate merger, right?

00:07.660 --> 00:09.960
Specifically in the global energy sector.

00:10.120 --> 00:12.380
Oh, wow. Okay. Set in stage big today.

00:12.540 --> 00:13.740
Yeah. Well, we have to.

00:14.080 --> 00:18.060
So two absolute giants of industry are combining forces.

00:18.460 --> 00:21.560
The board has signed off. The regulators, you know, they've given their blessing.

00:22.120 --> 00:24.700
The financial modeling is done. The champagne has been popped.

00:25.140 --> 00:27.800
They are completely ready to merge their operations.

00:27.800 --> 00:31.100
And of course, that means merging their most valuable assets.

00:31.360 --> 00:36.660
And for a modern energy company, I mean, those assets are no longer just like the physical drilling rigs, right?

00:36.760 --> 00:39.900
The pipeline infrastructure. The true value is the data.

00:40.320 --> 00:45.360
Decades of it. We are talking about high resolution seismic surveys that have been mapped out over 50 years.

00:45.900 --> 00:52.760
Complex geological interpretations, drilling records, basically petabyte scale well logs.

00:52.880 --> 00:54.560
Yeah. A staggering amount of information.

00:54.560 --> 01:02.040
Exactly. But when the newly merged IQ organization goes to actually integrate this data, they hit this impenetrable wall.

01:02.380 --> 01:04.880
This multibillion dollar merger is suddenly paralyzed.

01:05.340 --> 01:06.720
The data is trapped, right?

01:07.060 --> 01:16.380
Yes. Entirely trapped in dozens of proprietary, completely incompatible databases that were built by competing vendors over the years.

01:16.380 --> 01:19.380
It's basically the ultimate translation problem in enterprise architecture.

01:20.060 --> 01:24.560
I mean, you have vendor A's system and vendor B's system, and they were built across entirely different decades.

01:24.760 --> 01:24.920
Right.

01:24.980 --> 01:30.960
They might not even share a common digital definition of what a well actually is at, you know, the schema level.

01:31.060 --> 01:32.740
Which is wild to think about.

01:32.780 --> 01:33.540
It really is.

01:33.540 --> 01:40.880
To merge these two corporations, you essentially have to merge two massive, completely disjointed digital universes.

01:40.880 --> 01:50.960
And bringing in any new analytics tool or like any modern machine learning model, that means paying a systems integrator to write yet another custom adapter.

01:51.160 --> 01:52.480
Yeah, which is expensive and slow.

01:52.740 --> 02:01.280
It's the architectural equivalent of trying to connect 100 different electrical appliances from 100 different countries, and absolutely none of them fit the wall outlet.

02:01.380 --> 02:02.280
That's a great way to put it.

02:02.280 --> 02:07.440
The data is priceless, but structurally, it's almost impossible to move or query holistically.

02:08.260 --> 02:15.220
So, today's mission on this deep dive is to unpack the industry's ultimate solution to this exact translation problem.

02:15.220 --> 02:18.660
A system called the OSDU SPI stack.

02:19.380 --> 02:22.040
And we are going to explore exactly what this stack is.

02:22.340 --> 02:28.720
The highly sophisticated and sometimes deliberately contradictory, which is fun engineering concepts that make it work.

02:29.200 --> 02:31.980
And fundamentally, why it has to exist in the first place.

02:31.980 --> 02:36.240
What makes this deep dive particularly fascinating is our source material today.

02:36.480 --> 02:41.640
We're working off an incredibly candid 34-record architectural decision register.

02:41.800 --> 02:42.800
Which is just a goldmine.

02:42.920 --> 02:43.620
It really is.

02:43.620 --> 02:48.040
Along with subsystem guides pulled straight from the project's own internal documentation.

02:48.240 --> 02:52.140
I mean, if you read enterprise architecture docs, you know they usually read like marketing brochures.

02:52.380 --> 02:52.800
Oh, absolutely.

02:52.900 --> 02:54.880
They smooth over all the rough edges.

02:55.080 --> 02:55.180
Right.

02:55.500 --> 02:57.220
But this text does the exact opposite.

02:57.220 --> 03:02.380
It ruthlessly documents its own operational gaps, its broken pathways, its compromises.

03:02.880 --> 03:06.500
Seeing this level of transparency is super rare in software engineering.

03:07.140 --> 03:12.500
I mean, the authors preserve their technical admissions because those admissions explain the why behind the system.

03:12.960 --> 03:15.200
They aren't just dictating what tools they chose.

03:15.320 --> 03:15.680
Exactly.

03:15.960 --> 03:23.620
They are laying bare the brutal engineering trade-offs required to make complex cloud systems function in reality.

03:23.620 --> 03:24.920
Okay, let's unpack this.

03:25.080 --> 03:26.440
Let's start with the macro environment.

03:27.040 --> 03:30.320
The energy industry is drowning in trapped data, as we established.

03:30.780 --> 03:31.800
Enter OSDU.

03:32.180 --> 03:37.080
For those evaluating data platforms, what exactly is the open subsurface data universe?

03:37.640 --> 03:39.820
Well, OSDU was developed under the Open Group.

03:40.300 --> 03:45.580
And it basically serves as the industry's collective open standard answer to this data paralysis.

03:45.820 --> 03:45.960
Right.

03:45.960 --> 03:48.680
But the architecture here is crucial to understand.

03:49.060 --> 03:50.720
It is not a centralized data lake.

03:50.920 --> 03:55.640
It's not just a massive hard drive in the cloud where everyone dumps their CSV and LES file.

03:55.740 --> 03:58.640
Yeah, it's not simply a storage bucket with a fancy label on it.

03:58.720 --> 03:59.240
Far from it.

03:59.660 --> 04:05.160
OSDU actually specifies an entire suite of microservices with rigorously defined APIs.

04:05.720 --> 04:07.420
It acts as a common data platform.

04:07.620 --> 04:08.880
So it defines the rules of the road.

04:09.160 --> 04:09.440
Yes.

04:09.820 --> 04:13.540
It defines a storage service that handles the physical records.

04:13.540 --> 04:19.460
It defines a schema service that dictates the exact JSON structure those records are permitted to have.

04:19.720 --> 04:21.940
Oh, so everyone has to speak the exact same language.

04:22.100 --> 04:22.420
Precisely.

04:22.820 --> 04:28.400
And there's a search service, an asynchronous indexing engine, a legal service to evaluate compliance tags.

04:28.700 --> 04:28.960
Yeah.

04:29.520 --> 04:36.840
An entitlement service mapping out authorization graphs, and even a partition service to achieve multi-tenant isolation.

04:37.300 --> 04:37.520
Okay.

04:37.520 --> 04:43.980
So if we go back to that travel adapter analogy from earlier, OSDU is basically the universal standard electrical outlet.

04:44.160 --> 04:44.440
Exactly.

04:44.620 --> 04:55.320
If your subsurface data is ingested into OSDU, any compatible application, whether it's an AI visualization tool or just a legacy dashboard, can simply plug in and read it.

04:55.440 --> 04:55.560
Right.

04:55.620 --> 05:00.020
Because you are no longer held captive by the single vendor who originally generated that data.

05:00.020 --> 05:03.400
You have one unified, agreed-upon API surface.

05:03.480 --> 05:04.520
Which sounds like a dream.

05:04.920 --> 05:08.680
But fulfilling that promise creates a massive secondary challenge, right?

05:08.760 --> 05:11.560
The documentation refers to it as the provider problem.

05:11.920 --> 05:12.200
Yes.

05:12.540 --> 05:13.560
The provider problem.

05:13.840 --> 05:16.380
Because OSDU was designed to be cloud agnostic.

05:16.580 --> 05:21.660
It has to run on AWS, Google Cloud, Azure, or even on-premises bare metal.

05:21.940 --> 05:26.640
But being purely platform agnostic at the application layer, that's kind of a myth, isn't it?

05:26.680 --> 05:27.920
Oh, it's absolutely a myth.

05:27.980 --> 05:28.140
Yeah.

05:28.140 --> 05:33.020
Because eventually, that application code has to write actual bytes to a physical disk.

05:33.100 --> 05:33.300
Right.

05:33.700 --> 05:37.840
Every single one of those microservices needs physical backing infrastructure.

05:38.460 --> 05:39.960
Records need a document database.

05:40.660 --> 05:44.380
The entitlement service needs a graph database to traverse user groups.

05:44.680 --> 05:45.980
And they need to talk to each other.

05:46.120 --> 05:46.480
Exactly.

05:46.660 --> 05:50.720
So the services need to communicate asynchronously, which requires a message broker.

05:51.100 --> 05:53.540
They all need identity providers and seekers management.

05:53.840 --> 05:57.340
And that infrastructure looks vastly different depending on your cloud provider.

05:57.340 --> 06:03.320
You can't just drop the same code into AWS and Azure and expect it to know how to talk to their specific databases.

06:03.560 --> 06:03.680
Right.

06:03.980 --> 06:07.640
So the OSDU architecture solves this by splitting the code base.

06:08.120 --> 06:14.820
The core business logic, the actual rules determining how a well log is parsed or how a legal tag is validated.

06:15.020 --> 06:15.880
That part is shared.

06:15.880 --> 06:21.200
But the cloud plumbing is abstracted behind swappable provider implementations.

06:21.600 --> 06:28.660
So if you look at the source tree for any OSDU service, you will see a directory called StorageCore containing the pure Java logic.

06:29.400 --> 06:31.720
And that sits alongside the specific cloud stuff.

06:31.840 --> 06:32.200
Exactly.

06:32.360 --> 06:37.080
It sits directly alongside directories like StorageAzure, StorageAz, and StorageGCP.

06:37.080 --> 06:39.020
The core dictates the rules.

06:39.440 --> 06:40.900
And the provider executes the plumbing.

06:41.400 --> 06:45.160
This is the classic service provider interface or SPI pattern.

06:45.340 --> 06:45.560
Bingo.

06:45.760 --> 06:50.140
And that brings us directly to the specific technology we are examining today in our deep dive.

06:50.720 --> 06:53.060
The OSDU SPI stack.

06:53.500 --> 07:02.820
So if OSDU provides the abstract blueprint and the Azure provider repository contains the specific code to interact with Azure, who actually bids the physical infrastructure?

07:02.820 --> 07:05.360
That is the exact gap the SPI stack fills.

07:05.940 --> 07:08.540
It is specifically the service provider interface stack for Azure.

07:08.780 --> 07:14.380
Its entire mandate is to take the Azure core and the Azure components of OSDU, wire them together, and deploy them.

07:14.480 --> 07:16.800
And it deliberately ignores every other cloud provider.

07:16.880 --> 07:17.720
Completely ignores them.

07:17.840 --> 07:18.860
It is Azure only.

07:19.240 --> 07:23.620
Now, the documentation points out a staggering operational reality here.

07:24.180 --> 07:31.580
The OSDU Azure provider code knows perfectly well how to query an existing Azure Cosmos DB instance, right?

07:31.580 --> 07:36.140
Or how to publish a message to an existing Azure service bus.

07:36.260 --> 07:36.420
Right.

07:36.500 --> 07:39.160
The application code knows how to talk to them once they exist.

07:39.400 --> 07:42.960
But the application code has absolutely no mechanism to create those resources.

07:43.680 --> 07:53.100
Getting from a completely empty Azure subscription to a fully functional OSDU environment manually is, well, the docs make it sound like a logistical nightmare.

07:53.220 --> 07:54.100
It's a total nightmare.

07:54.280 --> 07:54.360
Yeah.

07:54.620 --> 07:57.260
Think about what is actually required for a manual deployment.

07:57.680 --> 08:01.000
You are provisioning roughly 50 independent Azure resources.

08:01.000 --> 08:01.440
50.

08:01.580 --> 08:01.980
Wow.

08:02.140 --> 08:02.320
Yeah.

08:02.920 --> 08:12.060
You have to establish virtual networks, configure subnets, set a private endpoints to prevent public internet routing, deploy Cosmos DB accounts, configure service bus namespaces.

08:12.120 --> 08:13.540
Just listing them is exhausting.

08:13.740 --> 08:14.280
I know, right?

08:14.500 --> 08:24.860
And then you have to provision an Azure Kubernetes service cluster, map role-based access control across EnterID, and finally execute a dozen complex bootstrapping scripts to inject the initial state.

08:24.860 --> 08:27.200
And if you miss a single step.

08:27.200 --> 08:33.780
Like, what if you misconfigure one subnet mask or assign a reader role where a contributor role was needed?

08:34.020 --> 08:36.360
The entire microservice mesh collapses.

08:36.760 --> 08:37.320
Unbelievable.

08:37.320 --> 08:40.720
The docs refer to this as a multi-day expert exercise.

08:40.720 --> 08:44.280
And the critical flaw isn't just the time it takes.

08:44.520 --> 08:46.540
It's the lack of determinism.

08:46.860 --> 08:49.060
What do you mean by determinism in this context?

08:49.060 --> 08:58.060
Well, if an engineer manually configures the cluster on Monday and a different engineer does it on Thursday, they're going to make slightly different micro decisions along the way.

08:58.260 --> 08:59.020
Oh, right.

08:59.220 --> 08:59.800
Human drift.

09:00.040 --> 09:00.460
Exactly.

09:00.680 --> 09:06.560
You end up with environments that exhibit bizarre, untraceable bugs simply because of human operational drift.

09:06.560 --> 09:09.680
So the SBI stack exists to eliminate that drift entirely.

09:10.000 --> 09:15.360
It takes that brittle multi-day export exercise and compresses it into a single automated execution.

09:15.620 --> 09:16.180
That's the goal.

09:16.420 --> 09:22.020
You open a terminal, authenticate to Azure, and run literally one command, spied up in vid one.

09:22.360 --> 09:23.020
Just one command.

09:23.320 --> 09:23.500
Yep.

09:24.020 --> 09:26.400
Then you wait approximately 45 to 50 minutes.

09:26.400 --> 09:36.420
And the vast majority of that time is just waiting for the Azure Resource Manager API to physically spin up the underlying Kubernetes nodes and databases.

09:36.640 --> 09:36.820
Right.

09:36.880 --> 09:38.720
Because hardware still takes time to allocate.

09:39.020 --> 09:39.420
Exactly.

09:39.800 --> 09:44.200
But when it finishes, you have a running, fully integrated OSDU environment.

09:44.420 --> 09:46.700
It's essentially an infrastructure magic trick.

09:46.700 --> 09:56.860
Under the hood, it's orchestrating a Python CLI, BICEP templates for the Azure Resource Graph, and Kubernetes manifests managed by a GitOps engine.

09:57.380 --> 10:01.860
But wait, here is where the architecture register throws a massive curveball.

10:01.940 --> 10:02.840
Oh, the production warning.

10:03.040 --> 10:03.340
Yes.

10:03.760 --> 10:08.520
The documentation is incredibly aggressive about stating what this 50-minute marvel is not.

10:08.640 --> 10:12.040
They state it plainly in bold text multiple times.

10:12.620 --> 10:14.100
This is not a production configuration.

10:14.580 --> 10:16.220
This is what I really want to push back on.

10:16.220 --> 10:19.800
Because, I mean, they engineered this incredibly sophisticated orchestration tool.

10:20.200 --> 10:23.580
They documented 34 complex architectural decisions to make it flawless.

10:24.040 --> 10:29.980
They automated a 50-minute deployment pipeline and then explicitly tell users they shouldn't run their business on it.

10:30.080 --> 10:32.280
Why build it at all if it's not meant for production?

10:32.600 --> 10:33.860
You have to look at the target audience.

10:34.500 --> 10:39.500
The primary users of the SPI stack are developers writing new features for OSDU,

10:40.160 --> 10:45.060
platform engineers evaluating whether OSDU on Azure fits their enterprise architecture,

10:45.060 --> 10:50.080
and CICD pipelines that need ephemeral environments to run integration tests.

10:50.600 --> 10:50.720
Okay.

10:50.840 --> 10:51.760
So it's a testing ground.

10:52.000 --> 10:52.380
Exactly.

10:52.700 --> 10:57.180
And when you design a system explicitly for dev, test, and continuous integration,

10:57.440 --> 11:00.140
your architectural priorities completely invert.

11:00.360 --> 11:01.220
Inverting priorities.

11:01.720 --> 11:03.960
Meaning you trade security for speed.

11:03.960 --> 11:10.220
More specifically, you trade strict isolation and disaster recovery for operational determinism and low latency provisioning.

11:10.340 --> 11:10.460
Okay.

11:10.560 --> 11:11.400
Break that down for me.

11:11.820 --> 11:15.760
In a true production environment, your top priority is zero-trust architecture.

11:16.280 --> 11:20.420
You want separate virtual networks, isolated managed identities for every single pod,

11:20.880 --> 11:23.960
cross-region replication, and automated credential rotation.

11:24.380 --> 11:26.240
Basically, you want maximum paranoia.

11:26.340 --> 11:26.520
Right.

11:26.740 --> 11:27.140
Exactly.

11:27.420 --> 11:28.300
Paranoia is good in production.

11:28.300 --> 11:33.100
But as the SPI stack, all OSDU services share a single Azure-managed identity.

11:33.680 --> 11:37.280
There are no automated backups for the databases running inside the cluster.

11:37.620 --> 11:37.980
Wow.

11:38.360 --> 11:44.160
So if a vulnerability in the search service allows an attacker to execute arbitrary code within that pod,

11:44.540 --> 11:50.840
they essentially inherit the permissions to read or destroy the Cosmos DB collections belonging to the storage service.

11:50.980 --> 11:51.280
Yes.

11:51.280 --> 11:55.000
Because both services are wearing the exact same Azure identity badge.

11:55.480 --> 11:56.000
Precisely.

11:56.500 --> 11:59.980
The blast radius of a compromise is the entire environment.

12:00.200 --> 12:01.240
Which sounds terrifying.

12:01.460 --> 12:01.940
It does.

12:02.500 --> 12:08.420
But for an ephemeral test environment that exists solely to validate a pull request and then destroy itself two hours later,

12:08.840 --> 12:11.760
that is a perfectly acceptable, even necessary trade-off.

12:11.820 --> 12:13.980
Because if you did it the production way, it would take too long.

12:13.980 --> 12:14.500
Exactly.

12:14.820 --> 12:24.940
If you forced a zero-trust model in a dev environment, you would spend 40 of those 50 minutes just waiting for Azure Enter ID to propagate role assignments for 30 different microservices.

12:25.120 --> 12:25.580
Oh, I see.

12:25.760 --> 12:30.580
The architecture here favors speed, predictability, and ease of teardown over paranoid isolation.

12:30.920 --> 12:32.000
That makes a lot of sense.

12:32.160 --> 12:38.520
To understand how the system avoids collapsing into chaos during that 50-minute window, though, we need to examine the boundaries.

12:39.460 --> 12:43.140
The documentation lays out this concept called the architecture of ownership.

12:43.140 --> 12:44.400
Yeah, this is brilliant.

12:44.600 --> 12:51.220
They divide the entire ecosystem into four distinct bosses, and each boss has strict jurisdictional boundaries.

12:51.780 --> 12:56.160
This separation of concerns is one of the most elegant concepts in the entire register.

12:56.460 --> 12:59.480
It prevents the orchestration tools from fighting each other.

13:00.060 --> 13:01.440
Let's break down those four owners.

13:01.720 --> 13:06.900
First, operating entirely outside the Kubernetes cluster, you have the CLI and the BICEP compiler.

13:07.240 --> 13:09.020
They own the Azure infrastructure.

13:09.020 --> 13:17.320
So their jurisdiction is the resource groups, the virtual network, the Cosmos DB accounts, the key vault, and the physical AKS cluster itself.

13:17.620 --> 13:19.280
And crucially, this layer is imperative.

13:19.860 --> 13:24.400
It only runs when a CI job or a human explicitly triggers that SPI-UP command.

13:24.480 --> 13:25.520
It does not run on a loop.

13:25.800 --> 13:26.280
Exactly.

13:26.580 --> 13:28.520
It provisions the foundation, and then it stops.

13:29.200 --> 13:33.620
Then, living strictly inside the Kubernetes cluster, you have boss number two, Flux.

13:33.880 --> 13:35.300
Flux is a GitOps engine, right.

13:35.300 --> 13:37.860
Yes, a GitOps continuous delivery engine.

13:38.360 --> 13:40.260
It owns the Kubernetes workloads.

13:40.880 --> 13:45.180
It reads YAML manifests from a Git repository and applies them to the cluster.

13:45.520 --> 13:49.080
So unlike the CLI, Flux is declarative and continuous.

13:49.500 --> 13:49.760
Right.

13:49.860 --> 13:54.000
It constantly watches the cluster state and ensures it matches the Git repository.

13:54.260 --> 13:55.300
Okay, so that's two bosses.

13:55.640 --> 13:58.240
Boss number three is the Kubernetes controllers and operators.

13:58.660 --> 14:02.200
These handle specific managed states for complex applications.

14:02.200 --> 14:02.720
Yeah.

14:03.220 --> 14:13.300
Like, if you deploy a database cluster inside Kubernetes, an operator is the specialized software loop that monitors that database, handles failovers, or issues TLS certificates.

14:13.500 --> 14:17.000
And the operators do their job regardless of what Flux or the CLI are doing.

14:17.160 --> 14:17.540
Exactly.

14:17.660 --> 14:18.560
They have their own little kingdom.

14:19.020 --> 14:21.500
And finally, boss number four is the human operator.

14:21.540 --> 14:21.980
Us.

14:22.260 --> 14:22.600
Us.

14:22.980 --> 14:25.420
The human owns the actual decision-making logic.

14:26.060 --> 14:29.780
When to bump a version, when to trigger a teardown, when to clear a cache.

14:29.780 --> 14:34.140
Now, the documentation points out a really important negative claim here regarding these boundaries.

14:34.860 --> 14:39.220
It explicitly warns that a successful CLI exit is not a readiness check.

14:39.340 --> 14:40.460
This trips up so many people.

14:40.820 --> 14:41.360
I can imagine.

14:41.740 --> 14:48.300
You can run speed up, watch the terminal scroll for 50 minutes, see a bright green success message, get your bash prompt back.

14:48.700 --> 14:54.020
But if you try to send an API request to OSDU right then, it will fail.

14:54.300 --> 14:55.280
It absolutely will fail.

14:55.340 --> 14:56.680
The system isn't actually ready yet.

14:56.680 --> 14:59.500
And they stress this is not a bug in the CLI.

14:59.760 --> 15:03.000
It is a fundamental reality of respecting those jurisdictional boundaries.

15:03.560 --> 15:05.900
The CLI's job is just infrastructure.

15:06.160 --> 15:06.240
Right.

15:06.500 --> 15:13.220
The moment the infrastructure is provisioned and Flux is installed, the CLI's jurisdiction ends and it reports success.

15:13.380 --> 15:14.060
It did its job.

15:14.160 --> 15:15.380
It's like a construction site.

15:15.540 --> 15:17.260
The CLI is the general contractor.

15:17.460 --> 15:23.160
They pour the foundation, frame the walls, wire the main electrical panels, hand you the keys to the front door, and drive away.

15:23.500 --> 15:24.520
Their contract is fulfilled.

15:24.520 --> 15:36.580
But when you unlock that door and walk inside, Flux, who is basically the interior decorator, is still furiously unpacking boxes, assembling furniture, painting the walls, and booting up the actual appliances.

15:37.080 --> 15:38.520
That analogy holds perfectly.

15:38.980 --> 15:46.220
Provisioning the Azure infrastructure and converging the Kubernetes workloads are genuinely separate concerns happening on completely separate timelines.

15:46.220 --> 15:53.520
And by refusing to artificially combine them, the project saves operators from misunderstanding the system state.

15:53.760 --> 15:54.140
Exactly.

15:54.600 --> 16:01.140
If an API request fails after the CLI finishes, you know you aren't debugging an Azure networking issue.

16:01.600 --> 16:04.960
You know you are waiting on a Kubernetes pod to pass its readiness probe.

16:05.520 --> 16:06.880
It isolates the troubleshooting.

16:06.880 --> 16:09.460
So the general contractor builds the foundation.

16:09.880 --> 16:13.520
Let's look at the actual architectural blueprint they are using to pour that concrete.

16:13.880 --> 16:19.160
In the decision register, this is called ADR001, the Azure only bet.

16:19.620 --> 16:21.980
This decision is the linchpin for the entire stack.

16:22.080 --> 16:23.720
Remember the provider problem we talked about?

16:23.820 --> 16:24.020
Right.

16:24.120 --> 16:26.720
Needing specific infrastructure for OSDU to run.

16:26.880 --> 16:27.180
Yes.

16:27.500 --> 16:31.700
OSDU requires document storage, object storage, messaging, and identity.

16:31.700 --> 16:35.960
When architecting a cloud-native platform, you generally face a fork in the road here.

16:36.200 --> 16:38.360
The portable route versus the cloud-native route.

16:38.620 --> 16:39.000
Exactly.

16:39.480 --> 16:47.160
The traditional portable approach is to run all of those dependencies as open source, stateful workloads directly inside your Kubernetes cluster.

16:47.680 --> 16:53.040
You deploy PostgreSQL for relational data, Minio for object storage, RabbitMQ for messaging.

16:53.260 --> 16:54.420
This is where I have to pause.

16:55.080 --> 17:00.620
Because if you talk to any modern platform engineer, the holy grail is portability.

17:00.620 --> 17:04.500
The whole point of adopting Kubernetes is to avoid vendor lock-in, right?

17:04.560 --> 17:05.220
That's theory, yeah.

17:05.340 --> 17:14.520
If you put all your state inside the cluster, you can theoretically lift and shift that entire cluster from Azure to AWS or Google Cloud with zero code changes.

17:15.100 --> 17:18.220
Why would the SPI stack deliberately abandon that portability?

17:18.460 --> 17:29.220
Because the SPI stack engineers analyzed the actual real-world cost of that theoretical portability, and they realized it was a terrible deal for their specific use case.

17:29.320 --> 17:29.840
How so?

17:29.840 --> 17:32.880
Running stateful systems inside Kubernetes is brutal.

17:33.420 --> 17:39.320
If you deploy a massive RabbitMQ cluster and a distributed PostgreSQL database inside your pods, you now own them.

17:39.460 --> 17:39.580
Right.

17:39.660 --> 17:40.680
You're responsible for them.

17:40.740 --> 17:41.720
You are deeply responsible.

17:41.860 --> 17:44.000
You have to configure the persistent volume claims.

17:44.380 --> 17:47.180
You have to write the cron jobs to back up the data to blob storage.

17:47.560 --> 17:51.060
You have to manage the memory limits, the read replicas, the failover elections.

17:51.300 --> 17:56.820
You essentially become a full-time database administrator just to keep your ephemeral dev environment alive.

17:56.820 --> 18:07.000
Exactly. For a platform designed for testing in CI pipelines, that is an enormous expensive operational tax to pay for a portability that you literally do not need.

18:07.000 --> 18:12.140
I mean, the entire purpose of the SPI stack is to test the OSDU Azure provider code.

18:12.240 --> 18:20.260
Right. That provider code was written specifically to interact with the proprietary APIs of Azure Cosmos DB and Azure Service Bus.

18:20.260 --> 18:27.000
If you deploy open source RabbitMQ instead of Service Bus, you aren't even executing the code you set out to test.

18:27.200 --> 18:32.300
That makes perfect sense. You'd be building a test environment that completely bypasses the subject under test?

18:32.420 --> 18:33.400
It would be totally pointless.

18:33.980 --> 18:35.600
So they made the pragmatic choice.

18:36.020 --> 18:41.000
They pushed the state out of Kubernetes and into Azure's platform as a service, or PANAS offerings.

18:41.000 --> 18:49.200
Yes. The stack relies heavily on managed Cosmos DB, managed Service Bus, managed storage accounts. It is Azure only by design.

18:49.420 --> 18:56.540
Moving it to AWS wouldn't just require changing a few Helm variables. It would require an entirely different orchestration stack.

18:56.840 --> 19:00.140
And the ripple effects of pushing state to Azure PANAS are massive.

19:00.880 --> 19:08.980
Because the cluster isn't burdened with running heavy database engines, the Kubernetes footprint can be much smaller, cheaper, and way faster to provision.

19:08.980 --> 19:13.460
But as with any strict architectural doctrine, reality forces compromises.

19:14.160 --> 19:16.180
Three systems were not moved to PANAS.

19:16.520 --> 19:19.920
The documentation dramatically calls them the three that stayed.

19:20.200 --> 19:21.420
It sounds like a movie title.

19:21.660 --> 19:23.800
But yeah, let's dissect those exceptions.

19:24.240 --> 19:28.400
Because the reasons they stayed behind are fascinating studies in technical constraints.

19:28.740 --> 19:29.600
Okay, first up.

19:30.240 --> 19:32.420
Elastic search stayed inside the cluster.

19:33.020 --> 19:35.160
Why not use a managed search service?

19:35.360 --> 19:36.060
Azure has one, right?

19:36.180 --> 19:37.320
They do. And it's great.

19:37.320 --> 19:41.440
But there was no compatible managed substitute for this specific use case.

19:42.060 --> 19:46.100
The OSDU search microservice is hard-coded to call the Elasticsearch API.

19:46.660 --> 19:48.860
It constructs complex Lucene queries.

19:49.080 --> 19:51.340
And Azure's managed search doesn't support that.

19:51.420 --> 19:58.820
Right. Azure offers a phenomenal managed service called Azure AI Search, but it does not speak the Elasticsearch API natively.

19:58.880 --> 19:59.300
Oh, I see.

19:59.300 --> 20:10.560
To swap them, you would have to fork the OSDU source code and completely rewrite the search module, which, as we just established, violates the SPI stack's mandate of testing the core code as is.

20:10.640 --> 20:13.460
You can't rewrite the app just to make the infrastructure happy.

20:13.640 --> 20:18.280
So Elasticsearch remains a heavy, stateful workload inside the cluster.

20:18.460 --> 20:18.820
Exactly.

20:18.820 --> 20:20.980
Okay. The second exception is Redis.

20:21.340 --> 20:28.420
Now, Azure absolutely provides a fully managed Azure cache for Redis, but the SPI stack runs Redis in cluster.

20:28.700 --> 20:28.980
Why?

20:29.380 --> 20:33.200
This was purely a judgment call based on cost, latency, and security overhead.

20:33.860 --> 20:39.680
The OSDU services require custom, certificate authority-backed TLS connections to Redis.

20:39.680 --> 20:41.600
Okay. So it's a secure connection.

20:41.760 --> 20:42.180
Very secure.

20:42.980 --> 20:49.060
But routing that traffic out of the cluster across the Azure backbone to a managed PS instance just to cache temporary tokens.

20:49.160 --> 20:52.880
That adds unnecessary network latency and a substantial monthly bill.

20:53.020 --> 20:59.420
So for a def-tested environment, spinning up a lightweight Redis pod inside the cluster is just vastly cheaper and performs better.

20:59.560 --> 21:01.320
Precisely. It wasn't worth the PS overhead.

21:01.620 --> 21:02.680
And the third one is Postgresful.

21:03.480 --> 21:05.760
Now, this one almost feels spiteful when you read the docs.

21:05.760 --> 21:13.940
Despite having massive, scalable, managed Postgresful offerings in Azure, they run it in cluster solely because of one tiny component.

21:14.200 --> 21:21.780
Yes. The Apache Airflow workflow engine used for ingestion requires a tiny relational database just to keep track of its own metadata.

21:22.140 --> 21:23.580
It just needs a tiny scratch pad.

21:23.640 --> 21:24.320
That's all it is.

21:24.320 --> 21:37.440
And paying for a highly available, externally managed Azure database for Postgresful instance, setting up the private endpoints, managing the firewall rules, all just to store a few megabytes of workflow state for Airflow.

21:37.640 --> 21:37.900
Oh.

21:37.980 --> 21:39.700
It's terrible engineering ROI.

21:39.700 --> 21:50.680
So despite the grand Azure-only PaaS-DIS doctrine, the cluster still ends up carrying around, what, 450 gigabytes of premium SSD storage just to satisfy these three specific systems?

21:50.880 --> 21:56.540
It really proves that rigid architectural purity rarely survives contact with legacy application requirements.

21:56.860 --> 22:04.520
Now, because they successfully pushed the vast majority of the state and complexity out to Azure PaaS, they took the next logical step.

22:04.520 --> 22:09.020
They let Azure take the wheel on managing the Kubernetes control plane itself.

22:09.480 --> 22:13.820
The STI stack utilizes a deployment model called AKS-Automatic.

22:14.040 --> 22:19.220
If you've ever managed a Kubernetes cluster, you know the control plane is notoriously complex.

22:19.360 --> 22:19.920
Oh, yeah.

22:20.100 --> 22:29.360
Traditionally, you have to manage the underlying virtual machine scale sets, configure the container network interface, deploy the service mesh, and write all the pod security policies.

22:29.940 --> 22:31.980
It is a relentless operational burden.

22:31.980 --> 22:35.340
And AKS-Automatic abstracts almost all of that away, right?

22:35.740 --> 22:39.540
Microsoft dictates the defaults, manages the nodes, and secures the platform.

22:40.160 --> 22:41.660
You trade control for peace of mind.

22:42.140 --> 22:43.300
But that trade is not free.

22:43.920 --> 22:47.300
When you hand over the keys to the platform, you have to live by the platform's rules.

22:47.880 --> 22:51.380
And AKS-Automatic enforces some incredibly strict guardrails.

22:51.600 --> 22:52.720
Tell me about rule number one.

22:52.960 --> 22:55.080
The first major constraint is the version floor.

22:55.720 --> 22:59.380
The SPI stack requires a minimum Kubernetes version of 1.36.

22:59.380 --> 23:01.640
Why that specific version?

23:01.840 --> 23:04.740
Is it tied to a specific Azure API release or something?

23:04.900 --> 23:07.980
It's tied to how AKS-Automatic handles cluster middleware.

23:08.720 --> 23:14.640
Below version 1.36, AKS-Automatic blocks the use of mutating admission webhooks.

23:15.080 --> 23:16.500
Okay, you'll have to explain what that is.

23:16.840 --> 23:19.200
Why is a mutating webhook a deal breaker?

23:19.200 --> 23:21.940
Let's look at the Kubernetes API server.

23:22.500 --> 23:27.080
When you submit a request to create a pod, the API server runs it through admission controllers

23:27.080 --> 23:28.760
before saving it to the database.

23:29.180 --> 23:34.600
A mutating webhook actually intercepts the JSON payload of your request and modifies it on

23:34.600 --> 23:40.260
the fly-like, injecting sidecar containers or altering configurations before it gets deployed.

23:40.520 --> 23:44.500
And those three stateful systems we just talked about, Elasticsearch, Redis, and Postgres,

23:44.500 --> 23:47.720
they rely on operators that use these webhooks to function.

23:47.860 --> 23:48.120
Exactly.

23:48.620 --> 23:52.520
Without the webhooks, the operators can't inject their TLS certificates or configure their

23:52.520 --> 23:55.200
storage volumes, and the entire middleware layer breaks.

23:55.380 --> 23:57.960
So version 1.36 is a hard floor.

23:58.240 --> 24:01.300
Okay, but the second constraint is where the engineering gets truly thorny.

24:01.860 --> 24:05.680
AKS-Automatic enforces mandatory deployment safeguards cluster-wide.

24:05.840 --> 24:06.180
Yes.

24:06.840 --> 24:12.400
Every single pod deployed to the cluster, with zero exceptions, must run as a non-root user.

24:12.400 --> 24:16.340
It must explicitly drop all Linux kernel capabilities.

24:16.820 --> 24:20.560
It must set exact CPU and memory requests and limits.

24:20.980 --> 24:23.140
It must block privilege escalation.

24:23.620 --> 24:26.320
Which sounds like fantastic security hygiene.

24:26.460 --> 24:28.200
Every enterprise should want that by default.

24:28.480 --> 24:29.000
They should.

24:29.500 --> 24:34.780
But the fatal flaw is that you are trying to deploy open-source OSDU community home charts.

24:35.040 --> 24:36.120
Oh, I see the problem.

24:36.280 --> 24:36.460
Right.

24:36.580 --> 24:41.020
These charts were written by developers spread across the globe who have likely never heard

24:41.020 --> 24:45.680
of this specific Azure policy, and they certainly didn't build their YAML templates to comply

24:45.680 --> 24:46.580
with it by default.

24:46.840 --> 24:48.160
So you have a fundamental collision.

24:48.380 --> 24:52.860
You have non-compliant community code that you are required to deploy onto a platform that

24:52.860 --> 24:55.320
strictly enforces compliance at the admission gate.

24:55.460 --> 24:55.620
Yep.

24:55.900 --> 25:01.360
If you try to deploy the community helm chart as is, the AKS API server will instantly reject

25:01.360 --> 25:01.520
it.

25:01.720 --> 25:04.840
The standard DevOps solution here is deployment time patching, right?

25:04.840 --> 25:10.160
You intercept the helm chart right before it hits the cluster, use a tool like customize

25:10.160 --> 25:14.320
to inject the missing security context fields, and pass it through.

25:14.860 --> 25:16.920
Why didn't the SPI stack just do that?

25:17.460 --> 25:20.220
Because deployment time patching is incredibly fragile.

25:21.000 --> 25:25.820
You are writing a patch that relies on the exact structure of the upstream YAML file.

25:25.980 --> 25:28.600
Meaning, if the structure changes, the patch breaks.

25:28.720 --> 25:29.360
Exactly.

25:29.360 --> 25:34.500
If the community updates their chart and moves a configuration block from line 10 to line 15,

25:34.680 --> 25:38.620
or renames a variable, your patch silently fails to apply.

25:39.080 --> 25:43.880
The deployment gets rejected, or far worse, it deploys without the security context you

25:43.880 --> 25:44.760
thought you were injecting.

25:44.980 --> 25:48.580
This leads to one of my absolute favorite architectural decisions in the whole stack.

25:48.920 --> 25:52.060
Instead of putting a bouncer at the door to check if everyone is wearing the proper safety

25:52.060 --> 25:56.620
gear, they just bought the factory and wove the safety gear directly into the uniforms.

25:56.740 --> 25:57.880
That is precisely what they did.

25:57.880 --> 26:03.080
They completely abandoned the upstream community helm charts for the OSDU microservices.

26:03.620 --> 26:10.060
Instead, the SPI stack engineers built one single locally maintained helm chart called Osdo

26:10.060 --> 26:10.860
Spy Service.

26:11.800 --> 26:18.720
Every single OSDU microservice storage search legal entitlements is deployed using this exact

26:18.720 --> 26:19.380
same chart.

26:19.760 --> 26:25.220
So the non-route security context, the resource limits, the dropped capabilities, everything

26:25.220 --> 26:27.140
is hard-coded directly into the template.

26:27.140 --> 26:28.680
It is compliant by construction.

26:29.040 --> 26:33.740
The policy is enforced at authoring time inside the artifact, completely eliminating the risk

26:33.740 --> 26:35.220
of drift during deployment.

26:35.540 --> 26:40.200
It's a brilliant application of the DRY principle, don't repeat yourself, applied to infrastructure

26:40.200 --> 26:40.740
security.

26:40.980 --> 26:43.100
But it introduces a new problem, doesn't it?

26:43.220 --> 26:43.640
It does.

26:43.880 --> 26:45.840
Because you aren't using the community charts.

26:46.280 --> 26:49.960
You can no longer track the community chart version numbers to know which version of the

26:49.960 --> 26:50.860
software you are running.

26:51.160 --> 26:54.280
You are forced to track the raw container image tags directly.

26:54.280 --> 27:24.280


27:24.280 --> 27:27.060
But it doesn't just throw everything at the API server simultaneously.

27:27.160 --> 27:28.480
It uses a dependency graph.

27:28.600 --> 27:30.320
A very strict dependency graph.

27:30.460 --> 27:32.380
It employs the core namespaces first.

27:33.000 --> 27:36.400
Then it deploys the node pool configurations and the operators.

27:37.140 --> 27:40.960
It waits for the operators to report healthy before deploying the databases.

27:41.700 --> 27:45.860
Only after the databases are ready does it attempt to deploy the OSDU microservices.

27:45.860 --> 27:48.280
It's a beautifully choreographed sequence.

27:48.460 --> 27:52.720
But here is the architectural decision that will make anyone familiar with GitOps stop

27:52.720 --> 27:53.460
in their tracks.

27:53.960 --> 27:55.140
The deliberate pause.

27:55.660 --> 27:56.560
Yeah, this is controversial.

27:56.820 --> 28:01.920
When the spig of command finishes its execution, its final act is to explicitly suspend the Git

28:01.920 --> 28:02.920
source in flux.

28:03.280 --> 28:06.180
If you are a GitOps purist, that sounds like heresy.

28:06.600 --> 28:10.760
The entire philosophical selling point of GitOps is continuous reconciliation.

28:10.760 --> 28:11.560
Right.

28:12.100 --> 28:16.880
You merge a pull request to the main branch and within 60 seconds, flux detects the change

28:16.880 --> 28:19.100
and automatically upbakes the cluster to match.

28:19.580 --> 28:21.940
The pipeline is meant to be constantly flowing.

28:22.140 --> 28:22.640
Constantly.

28:22.800 --> 28:27.980
So pausing GitOps feels like disabling your antivirus software right after you install it.

28:28.500 --> 28:34.100
If you suspend the source, aren't you just going back to the dark ages of static manual deployments?

28:34.740 --> 28:40.440
Why build a complex GitOps pipeline only to turn off the very continuous reconciliation it's famous for?

28:40.440 --> 28:45.440
To understand why, you have to put yourself in the shoes of a developer actively using this environment.

28:46.040 --> 28:50.300
Imagine you are a developer and you've been debugging a highly complex intermittent failure

28:50.300 --> 28:52.900
in the indexing service for the past 45 minutes.

28:53.000 --> 28:54.000
Okay, I'm stressed already.

28:54.320 --> 28:58.940
You have multiple terminal windows open, you are tracing logs across three different microservices,

28:59.220 --> 29:01.980
and you are finally zeroing in on the root cause.

29:02.660 --> 29:07.460
Meanwhile, on the other side of the company, a colleague merges an entirely unrelated pull request

29:07.460 --> 29:10.120
to the main branch of the infrastructure repository.

29:10.120 --> 29:11.800
Oh, I see where this is going.

29:12.240 --> 29:16.160
If continuous reconciliation is active, Flux sees that new commit.

29:16.580 --> 29:21.080
It immediately reaches out to the cluster and updates the environment right underneath the developer.

29:21.320 --> 29:23.460
Pods restart, configuration shift.

29:23.620 --> 29:24.000
Exactly.

29:24.360 --> 29:29.680
The bug the developer was chasing might vanish or morph into a completely different error,

29:29.860 --> 29:33.400
and they have absolutely no idea why the ground just shifted beneath them.

29:33.400 --> 29:37.680
It's the equivalent of a forensic detective dusting for fingerprints at a crime scene,

29:37.820 --> 29:42.680
and the janitorial staff walks in and starts mopping the floor because it's their scheduled cleaning time.

29:42.780 --> 29:43.800
That is exactly it.

29:44.040 --> 29:47.960
By suspending the git source, the SPI stack freezes the crime scene.

29:48.180 --> 29:50.860
And the decision register is very precise about the mechanics here.

29:51.280 --> 29:55.920
Suspending the git source simply tells Flux to stop fetching new commits from GitHub.

29:56.480 --> 29:59.120
It does not mean stop reconciling the cluster.

29:59.120 --> 30:03.940
That's a crucial distinction. Flux is still actively monitoring the Kubernetes API.

30:04.760 --> 30:10.660
If a frustrated developer manually SSHs into the cluster and deletes a critical config map just to see what happens,

30:11.020 --> 30:14.500
Flux will instantly replace it using the locally cached version of the repository.

30:15.160 --> 30:16.840
So it still heals manual tampering.

30:17.080 --> 30:21.080
It just refuses to introduce new upstream variables while you are working.

30:21.280 --> 30:24.960
Right. It is an incredibly thoughtful, developer-centric design choice.

30:24.960 --> 30:30.960
But even with the crime scene frozen, Kubernetes is a complex beast and deployments can still fail.

30:31.400 --> 30:37.460
The documentation highlights two specific ways reconciliations get permanently stuck in a GitOps flow,

30:37.620 --> 30:39.500
and the mechanisms behind them are fascinating.

30:39.940 --> 30:42.260
Let's talk about the first one, retry exhaustion.

30:42.600 --> 30:47.260
So when Flux attempts to install a helm release, the underlying pod fails to start.

30:47.880 --> 30:50.540
Maybe it's crash-lipping due to a bad configuration helm.

30:50.840 --> 30:51.900
We'll try again a few times.

30:51.960 --> 30:52.320
Makes sense.

30:52.320 --> 30:55.300
But eventually, it hits a retry limit and gives up.

30:56.040 --> 30:58.980
The helm controller enters a stalled state and permanently stops trying.

30:59.900 --> 31:03.700
The counterintuitive part for operators is that if you go into the Git repository

31:03.700 --> 31:08.880
and tell Flux to just apply the exact same manifest again, Flux ignores you.

31:09.000 --> 31:10.760
Because nothing changed in the file.

31:11.020 --> 31:13.900
Exactly. GitOps engines only react to deletes.

31:14.660 --> 31:19.460
If the file hasn't changed, Flux assumes its job is done, leaving the system stalled forever.

31:19.460 --> 31:23.800
So the SPI stack authors built a custom command, spy reconcile.

31:24.300 --> 31:31.140
Under the hood, this command reaches into the cluster and forcefully slaps a new timestamp annotation onto the stalled helm release object.

31:31.320 --> 31:36.960
It essentially tricks Flux into thinking the object has been modified, forcing it to attempt the deployment one more time.

31:37.040 --> 31:38.300
It's a very pragmatic workaround.

31:38.300 --> 31:45.680
But the second failure mode, immutable field drift, is genuinely mind-bending because it involves two automated systems fighting each other?

31:45.880 --> 31:47.040
Oh, this one is wild.

31:47.360 --> 31:52.380
This failure mode stems directly from those strict AKS automatic deployment safeguards we discussed earlier.

31:52.520 --> 31:54.540
Okay, remind me how those work in this context.

31:54.540 --> 31:58.680
In Kubernetes, certain fields within a pod template are immutable.

31:59.400 --> 32:01.920
You absolutely cannot change them once the object is created.

32:02.660 --> 32:06.180
Now, one of the Azure webhooks enforces a minimum CPU request.

32:06.920 --> 32:11.300
If a pod attempts to deploy requesting less than 100 millicores of CPU,

32:11.880 --> 32:16.520
the webhook intercepts the request and silently bumps it up to exactly 100 millicores.

32:16.700 --> 32:19.280
Okay, so the platform enforces a minimum baseline.

32:19.520 --> 32:21.140
That seems harmless enough.

32:21.140 --> 32:24.180
It seems harmless until you introduce Flux doing server-side apply.

32:24.480 --> 32:25.780
Let's trace the loop here.

32:26.180 --> 32:27.800
Flux reads the manifest from Git.

32:28.160 --> 32:30.840
The Git manifest requests 50 millicores.

32:31.020 --> 32:31.400
Got it.

32:31.740 --> 32:33.360
Flux submits this to the API server.

32:33.960 --> 32:38.700
The Azure webhook intercepts it, changes it to 100 millicores, and saves it to the etc. database.

32:39.280 --> 32:41.500
A few seconds later, Flux runs this reconciliation loop.

32:41.820 --> 32:44.320
It looks at the five cluster state, sees 100 millicores.

32:44.480 --> 32:46.700
It looks at the Git repository, sees 50 millicores.

32:46.800 --> 32:47.280
Oh, no.

32:47.480 --> 32:48.900
Flux immediately detects drift.

32:49.040 --> 32:49.300
Yes.

32:49.300 --> 32:51.560
Flux says, hey, someone tampered with this pod.

32:51.700 --> 32:52.780
I need to fix it back to 50.

32:52.940 --> 32:55.180
So Flux submits a patch to change it back to 50.

32:55.340 --> 33:00.000
But because pod templates are immutable, the Kubernetes API server outright rejects the patch.

33:00.340 --> 33:02.660
And the helm release completely wedges.

33:02.780 --> 33:04.560
It becomes an endless, unwinnable fight.

33:05.400 --> 33:08.040
Flux is desperately trying to enforce the Git truth of 50,

33:08.580 --> 33:12.580
and Azure is using the webhook to enforce the platform truth of 100.

33:13.080 --> 33:17.320
You can't force the patch through, and deleting the pod just starts the cycle over again.

33:17.320 --> 33:25.000
So how did the engineering team resolve an architectural standoff between the deployment engine and the platform security layer?

33:25.200 --> 33:26.620
By surrendering to reality.

33:27.340 --> 33:35.700
The engineers went into the local OSDU spy service helm chart and hard-coded the default CPU request to be exactly 100 millicores.

33:35.700 --> 33:38.060
They just gave up and gave Azure what it wanted.

33:38.320 --> 33:44.460
They deliberately wrote the code to match precisely what the platform webhook was going to force upon them anyway.

33:44.700 --> 33:49.680
It needs to reach out and talk to the Cosmos DB, and it needs to accept incoming requests from users.

33:50.220 --> 33:51.960
This brings us to identity.

33:52.640 --> 33:55.120
And the document makes a really forceful point here.

33:55.560 --> 33:57.720
Identity is two completely different jobs.

33:57.720 --> 34:02.340
This is arguably the most common pitfall for cloud engineers transitioning to microservices.

34:02.920 --> 34:04.600
They conflate authentication mechanisms.

34:05.180 --> 34:07.840
In this architecture, they are entirely separate workflows.

34:08.400 --> 34:11.000
There's outbound identity and inbound identity.

34:11.500 --> 34:12.840
Let's define that boundary clearly.

34:13.360 --> 34:14.980
Outbound identity answers the question.

34:15.360 --> 34:22.900
Can this specific Kubernetes pod get an access token to talk to an Azure backend service, like Cosmos DB or Key Vault?

34:22.900 --> 34:31.080
Right. And inbound identity answers the question, will this specific API request from an end user be accepted by the OSDU application logic?

34:31.180 --> 34:35.520
Exactly. You can have a pod that is perfectly authenticated to talk to the database on the backend.

34:36.040 --> 34:45.300
Outbound is flawless, but it rejects every single incoming user request with a 401 unauthorized error because the inbound identity configuration is wrong.

34:45.420 --> 34:50.140
And if you don't realize they are separate systems, you will spend hours tracing logs in the completely wrong layer.

34:50.140 --> 34:52.580
Let's start by unpacking outbound identity.

34:52.900 --> 34:56.660
The documentation refers to this section as secrets that do not exist.

34:57.220 --> 35:09.280
Traditionally, if a web app needs to query a database, the developer generates a connection string or a password, saves it in a configuration file, or maybe stores it in a Kubernetes secret, and the app reads it on startup.

35:09.580 --> 35:14.120
But any stored secret, even in a secure vault, is a massive operational liability.

35:14.360 --> 35:16.700
It can accidentally leak into application logs.

35:16.700 --> 35:18.860
It can be captured in a diagnostic screenshot.

35:18.860 --> 35:26.320
Most importantly, passwords must be rotated regularly, which requires complex automation to restart pods without dropping traffic.

35:26.620 --> 35:32.520
So the SPI stack completely eliminates connection strings using a protocol called Azure Workload Identity.

35:32.780 --> 35:32.980
Yes.

35:33.020 --> 35:34.100
Walk me through the mechanics of that.

35:34.180 --> 35:36.960
How does a pod query a database if it doesn't have a password?

35:36.960 --> 35:41.820
It relies on a federated chain of trust using OpenID Connect, or OIDC.

35:42.360 --> 35:45.940
First, the Kubernetes cluster itself acts as an identity provider.

35:46.360 --> 35:53.060
When a pod boots up, the Kubernetes AKI server mounts a signed, short-lived cryptographic token directly into the pod's file system.

35:53.280 --> 36:01.000
That token essentially says, I am the Kubernetes cluster, and I cryptographically vouch that this pod is running under service account X.

36:01.000 --> 36:06.360
Okay, so the pod has a voucher, but Azure Cosmos DB doesn't know what a Kubernetes service account is.

36:06.500 --> 36:06.800
Correct.

36:07.240 --> 36:16.420
So separately, the infrastructure automation goes into Microsoft EnterID, which is Azure's central identity system, and configures a federated credential.

36:16.780 --> 36:17.440
What does that do?

36:17.440 --> 36:32.460
You are essentially telling EnterID, if you ever receive a valid token from this specific Kubernetes cluster's OIDC endpoint, vouching for service account X, I want you to trust it and allow that pod to act as this specific Azure-managed identity.

36:32.860 --> 36:41.260
So the workflow is, the pod takes its Kubernetes voucher, hands it to EntraID, EntraID verifies the signature, and hands back a real Azure access token.

36:41.360 --> 36:41.720
Exactly.

36:41.720 --> 36:52.180
The pod then uses that Azure token to query Cosmos DB. There is literally no password stored anywhere in the cluster. It's just machines passing cryptographically signed, ephemeral vouchers back and forth.

36:52.300 --> 37:01.720
It's incredibly secure. But earlier we talked about how this stack makes explicit trade-offs because it's a dev environment. Here is where that trade-off manifests physically.

37:02.440 --> 37:03.800
Oh, the shared identity thing.

37:03.800 --> 37:10.500
Right. In a ZeoTrust production environment, you would create a separate Azure-managed identity for every single microservice.

37:10.500 --> 37:19.380
The storage service gets one identity that only has access to the storage collections. The search service gets a different identity that only has access to the search index.

37:19.540 --> 37:20.920
The principle of least privilege.

37:21.180 --> 37:31.960
Exactly. But managing a massive interconnected matrix of federated credentials for dozens of microservices in an ephemeral environment that only lives for an hour is a logistical nightmare.

37:32.420 --> 37:36.800
The BICEP templates would take twice as long to execute just waiting for role assignments to propagate.

37:36.800 --> 37:48.540
So the SPI stack makes a bold, controversial choice. All the OSDU services share one massive managed identity called Spustack Ostadu identity. It holds the keys to the entire kingdom.

37:48.840 --> 37:53.800
Cosmos DB, service bus, storage accounts, Key Vault. It has access to absolutely everything.

37:53.800 --> 38:11.720
And the decision register is refreshingly blunt about this reality. It openly admits that this architecture completely sacrifices back-end isolation. If a threat actor finds a remote code execution vulnerability in the legal service, they don't just have access to the legal tags.

38:11.720 --> 38:21.560
No, they can read the seismic data, drop the indexing cues, and wipe the Key Vault because Azure sees all the pods as the exact same highly privileged user.

38:21.800 --> 38:27.240
It is a deliberate choice prioritizing operational simplicity and deployment speed over zero trust security.

38:27.660 --> 38:36.900
It is a perfectly valid engineering choice for an internal CI testing target, but it would be a catastrophic architecture for a production environment hosting customer data.

38:36.900 --> 38:37.900
Absolutely catastrophic.

38:37.900 --> 38:45.880
And speaking of candid admissions, the documentation details a scenario where enforcing this modern identity pattern actually breaks legacy code.

38:46.300 --> 38:55.560
Let's talk about the broken async path. I found this section fascinating because it illustrates the real-world friction of trying to push modern security standards onto community code bases.

38:55.980 --> 39:06.440
In the OSDU architecture, data ingestion is highly asynchronous. When a user uploads a new well log, the storage service saves the file and then publishes an event message to the Azure service bus.

39:06.440 --> 39:06.880
Right.

39:06.880 --> 39:15.600
Another microservice, called IndexerQ, is supposed to pick up that message, parse the file, and send the metadata to Elasticsearch so the record becomes searchable.

39:15.960 --> 39:26.140
It's a standard event-driven architecture, but on a default SPI stack deployment, this entire asynchronous pathway is just broken. It fails silently out of the box.

39:26.260 --> 39:26.460
Yeah.

39:26.600 --> 39:31.480
And the root cause is hilarious in a dark systems engineering kind of way.

39:31.480 --> 39:42.980
Because the SPI stack rigidly enforces Azure workload identity, it intentionally disables password authentication on the Azure service bus namespace. It refuses to generate connection strings.

39:43.120 --> 39:44.020
Which is good security.

39:44.240 --> 39:52.640
It is. However, the community-provided container image for the IndexerQ service is older, and it hasn't been updated to support token-based authentication yet.

39:53.200 --> 39:58.440
On startup, the application code blindly searches its configuration files for a connection string password.

39:58.440 --> 40:03.620
And what does the SPI stack inject into the configuration file where that password is supposed to go?

40:03.780 --> 40:07.300
It literally injects the hard-coded string. Disabled.

40:07.940 --> 40:23.680
That's so funny. So the IndexerQ pod boots up, reads the word disabled, tries to use that literal word as a cryptographic password to authenticate against the service bus, fails completely, and the entire asynchronous ingestion path dies.

40:23.680 --> 40:34.000
And the authors just document this. They could have easily written a script to quietly re-enable passwords for that one specific service to make the environment work seamlessly out of the box.

40:34.180 --> 40:39.460
But they refuse to compromise their security architecture just to accommodate a legacy image constraint.

40:39.800 --> 40:46.800
They force the failure, document it clearly, and wait for the community repository to update their code to support modern identity standards.

40:47.020 --> 40:49.680
It is a highly principled, if frustrating, stance.

40:49.680 --> 40:55.360
Okay, so that covers the outbound identity. The pods can mostly talk to Azure. Let's pivot to inbound identity.

40:55.820 --> 40:59.240
And this involves a genuinely bizarre legacy application requirement.

40:59.240 --> 41:04.560
The core OSDU microservices are written in Java, primarily using the Spring Boot framework.

41:05.380 --> 41:15.260
When an API request comes into the cluster from an end user, the Java application is hard-coded to expect the user's identity to be plainly written in an HTTP header called XApp.

41:15.380 --> 41:22.160
And due to how the middleware is structured, you cannot easily toggle this requirement off in the application properties.

41:22.160 --> 41:23.020
You really can't.

41:23.020 --> 41:37.100
Wait. So if I have a perfectly valid, cryptographically signed JSON web token, a JWT, and I attach it to my API request, the Java app will reject me if I didn't also manually write my email address into an HTTP header.

41:37.220 --> 41:39.380
Basically, yes. Your JWT is flawless.

41:39.940 --> 41:47.940
But the Spring Boot application will parse the headers, see that XApp is missing, log an error about an empty identity, and return a 401 unauthorized.

41:47.940 --> 42:00.060
But if the application is blindly trusting the value of an HTTP header, what stops a malicious user from just writing SuperAdmin into the XApp ad header, attaching it to their request, and bypassing the authorization checks entirely?

42:00.420 --> 42:04.280
That is the exact security vulnerability the architecture had to mitigate.

42:04.780 --> 42:09.160
They couldn't change the Java code, so they pushed the solution out to the service mesh layer.

42:09.360 --> 42:10.040
How did he do that?

42:10.300 --> 42:13.700
The SPI stack utilizes Istio as its ingress gateway.

42:13.700 --> 42:22.480
They implement a strict, three-step interception process at the edge of the cluster, long before the HTTP request ever touches the Java application pod.

42:22.900 --> 42:28.060
First, the Istio gateway intercepts the request and cryptographically validates the JWT.

42:28.540 --> 42:33.520
It checks the signature against the issuer's public keys to guarantee the token hasn't been tampered with.

42:33.800 --> 42:38.320
This is the bouncer at the front door holding your driver's license up to a blacklight to make sure it's not a fake.

42:38.560 --> 42:39.120
Exactly.

42:39.720 --> 42:41.340
Step two is where the magic happens.

42:41.340 --> 42:47.340
Inside the Envoy proxy, which is the data plane of Istio, they execute a small, custom LUA script.

42:47.900 --> 42:49.380
This script runs in microseconds.

42:49.560 --> 42:50.420
What does this script do?

42:50.820 --> 42:54.640
It extracts the verified user identity claims from the validated JWT payload.

42:55.240 --> 43:02.360
Then, it forcefully overwrites whatever XApp header the user originally sent, replacing it with the true identity extracted from the token.

43:02.360 --> 43:17.820
Oh, wow. So, the LUA script acts like a bouncer who physically rips off whatever fake name tag you tried to wear into the club, verifies your cryptographically signed ID, and prints you a brand new, unforgeable name tag right at the door before letting you walk inside.

43:17.820 --> 43:24.660
And that is exactly why the Java application can safely rely on an inherently insecure mechanism like an HTTP header.

43:24.660 --> 43:29.140
It knows the Istio bouncer at the edge of the network guarantees its accuracy.

43:29.660 --> 43:37.480
It is a very clever, highly performant workaround to bridge modern edge security with inflexible legacy application logic.

43:37.580 --> 43:40.000
Okay. So, let's summarize the state of the system right now.

43:40.400 --> 43:43.040
The general contractor has built the Azure infrastructure.

43:43.500 --> 43:46.100
Flux, the interior decorator, has deployed the workloads.

43:46.100 --> 43:48.180
GitOps is paused to protect the state.

43:48.640 --> 43:51.140
Outbound identity is wired up via federated credentials.

43:51.520 --> 43:54.060
Inbound identity is secured via Envoy LUA scripts.

43:54.420 --> 43:56.120
Every health probe is glowing green.

43:56.500 --> 43:58.200
The API responds to requests.

43:58.460 --> 44:02.140
And yet, despite all of that, the system is completely and utterly useless.

44:02.520 --> 44:02.860
Right.

44:03.460 --> 44:08.620
An empty OSDU deployment, out of the box, cannot accept a single record of data.

44:09.200 --> 44:10.420
It is functionally brain dead.

44:10.900 --> 44:14.360
To make it operational, you have to execute a bootstrapping phase.

44:14.360 --> 44:21.080
And the system requires three very specific sets of metadata before it will even process a basic ingestion request.

44:21.320 --> 44:23.160
First, it needs a partition record.

44:23.680 --> 44:26.200
The OSDU architecture is inherently multi-tenant.

44:26.840 --> 44:31.940
Every single time a microservice receives a request, it has to look at the headers and ask the partition service,

44:32.240 --> 44:38.660
hey, which specific Cosmos DB collection and which service bus topic belong to this specific tenant?

44:38.860 --> 44:41.020
And if there are no partitions defined in the database?

44:41.320 --> 44:43.740
No service knows where its backend resources are located.

44:43.740 --> 44:43.980
Okay.

44:44.200 --> 44:46.420
Second, it needs entitlements root groups.

44:46.800 --> 44:50.460
Every action in OSDU requires an authorization check against a graph database.

44:50.880 --> 44:55.800
If there are no administrative groups pre-populated in the system, every single authorization check fails.

44:55.980 --> 45:01.740
Not because you are actively denied access, but because the system literally has no graph data to evaluate against.

45:01.740 --> 45:04.700
And third, it requires default legal tags.

45:05.380 --> 45:08.000
OSDU enforces strict data governance.

45:08.580 --> 45:16.840
You physically cannot ingest a seismic survey or a well log without tagging it with a legal policy dictating its data residency and expiration date.

45:16.840 --> 45:23.140
If no baseline policies exist in the legal service, the ingestion pipeline rejects the data outright.

45:23.260 --> 45:28.040
And beyond those three operational requirements, it also needs an enormous dictionary of schemas.

45:28.040 --> 45:41.160
The documentation notes that an empty system needs 1,386 different JSON schemas loaded into its database just to recognize standard industry data types like well bore trajectory or seismic trace data.

45:41.160 --> 45:46.360
In older legacy architectures, this bootstrapping problem was solved with a fragile CLI script.

45:46.920 --> 45:56.400
An engineer would deploy the infrastructure, wait for it to stabilize, and then run a bash script from their laptop that fired thousands of sequential HTTP API calls to populate this data.

45:56.480 --> 46:04.280
Which is a terrible operational pattern because it relies on human memory to execute, and it happens completely outside the purview of the infrastructure as code state.

46:04.620 --> 46:07.900
If the script fails halfway through, you have a partially corrupted environment.

46:07.900 --> 46:12.680
So the SBI stack team moved to a paradigm called bootstrap as data.

46:13.220 --> 46:13.980
How does that work?

46:14.580 --> 46:27.680
They engineered one-shot Kubernetes jobs, managed by Flux, that run exactly once during the deployment sequence to initialize these partitions, entitlements, and schemas directly against the internal APIs.

46:27.820 --> 46:28.420
That's smart.

46:28.420 --> 46:38.320
The brilliance of this approach is that the Kubernetes cluster itself now permanently holds the state of, has this data been loaded, in the form of a completed job object.

46:38.620 --> 46:42.840
You don't have to check external logs or query the database to know if bootstrapping finished.

46:43.040 --> 46:44.420
The cluster state is self-documenting.

46:44.420 --> 46:52.520
But wait, how do they ensure that all these moving parts, the core microservices, the bootstrap jobs, the schema loaders, are perfectly aligned on their versions?

46:52.880 --> 46:58.540
Because if you were just pulling the latest tag from a community Docker registry, you are asking for a disaster.

46:58.980 --> 47:02.560
Relying on the latest tag is the cardinal sin of reproducible deployments.

47:02.560 --> 47:09.560
If you use latest, the underlying container image might be updated by the community midway through your CI test suite.

47:09.940 --> 47:16.980
Your environment shifts underneath you, which violates the entire reason they went through the trouble of pausing the GitOps reconciliation in the first place.

47:17.520 --> 47:24.500
Right. The obvious fix is to hardcode the specific immutable version tag in every single deployment manifest.

47:24.880 --> 47:26.780
But that creates a massive maintenance burden.

47:26.780 --> 47:38.700
If you have 14 different microservices and you want to bump the release version from v0210 to v0211, you have to submit a Git commit that touches a dozen different YAML files scattered across the repository.

47:39.080 --> 47:43.760
It's noisy, it pollutes the Git history, and it's highly prone to copy-paste errors.

47:43.900 --> 47:48.100
To solve this, the SPI stack introduced a mechanism they call the image lock.

47:48.440 --> 47:49.580
Yes, the image lock.

47:49.900 --> 47:54.200
It is a single, centralized config map file in Kubernetes named osdo image lock.

47:54.200 --> 48:04.180
This single file holds the exact repository URL, the release tag, and crucially, the cryptographic SHA-256 digest for all the container images used in the system.

48:04.440 --> 48:09.940
So, when Flux deploys a microservice, it doesn't look for an image tag in the deployment YAML.

48:10.120 --> 48:12.080
No. Instead, it points to a variable.

48:12.560 --> 48:16.820
And Flux substitutes the exact digest pulled from that central lock file.

48:16.820 --> 48:25.460
It acts exactly like a packaged lock.json file in Node.js web development, but scaled up to manage massive cluster infrastructure.

48:25.900 --> 48:28.980
It creates a single, version-controlled source of truth.

48:29.400 --> 48:32.560
If you want to update the environment, you only modify one file.

48:32.760 --> 48:33.260
Exactly.

48:33.480 --> 48:40.860
And this seemingly mundane lock file is actually the secret weapon that allows this entire stack to fulfill its ultimate, hidden purpose.

48:41.060 --> 48:46.340
Which brings us to the most complex and operationally fascinating part of the architecture, the backing environment.

48:46.340 --> 48:54.160
Right. Because everything we have discussed so far, the BICEP, the Flux, the Identity, the Bootstrapping, is just about deploying OSDU.

48:54.620 --> 48:57.380
But the SPI stack isn't just a deployment utility.

48:57.840 --> 49:02.720
It serves as a shared, centralized test target for eight independent software repositories.

49:03.120 --> 49:06.120
Think about the massive scale of the CICD requirements here.

49:06.680 --> 49:12.760
Microsoft maintains active forks of the core OSDU services to inject Azure-specific optimizations.

49:12.760 --> 49:18.260
They have separate repositories for storage, search, legal entitlements, and so on.

49:18.580 --> 49:26.040
Every single time a developer opens a pull request on any one of those eight repositories, they need absolute proof that their code works.

49:26.180 --> 49:34.940
They need to know, does my new C-sharp or Java code actually communicate correctly with a live Cosmos DB and a live service bus?

49:34.940 --> 49:38.740
You cannot just run mocked unit tests on a developer laptop to prove that.

49:38.960 --> 49:43.900
Network latency, identity propagation, database throughput, those only emerge in a real environment.

49:44.160 --> 49:50.120
But we established at the beginning of this deep dive that it takes 50 minutes and costs tangible Azure compute money to stand up an environment.

49:50.420 --> 49:57.980
You cannot physically spin up a brand new, isolated SPI stack from scratch every single time a developer pushes a commit across eight different repositories.

49:57.980 --> 50:01.780
No, it would take hours to run a simple test, and the cloud bill would be astronomical.

50:01.780 --> 50:07.660
So they solved the scaling problem by using one single shared standing environment.

50:08.120 --> 50:16.080
All eight repositories deploy their unmerged, untested code into this one persistent environment all day long.

50:16.300 --> 50:18.480
This is where my operational alarms start ringing.

50:18.480 --> 50:29.480
If eight different development teams are autonomously deploying their unmerged code into a single shared environment simultaneously, how does it not instantly devolve into absolute chaos?

50:29.840 --> 50:34.080
Right. How are they not constantly overwriting each other's changes and permanently breaking the platform?

50:34.240 --> 50:39.380
It requires extreme programmatic coordination, and it starts with the foundation of the environment itself.

50:40.120 --> 50:47.100
The shared standing environment is deliberately pinned to a static, known good release tag of the SPI stack infrastructure.

50:47.100 --> 50:50.740
It never tracks a rolling branch like main or develop.

50:50.860 --> 50:59.300
Because if the main environment was constantly updating its core infrastructure, like upgrading the Kubernetes version or changing the Istio configuration and a bug was introduced,

50:59.820 --> 51:05.140
all eight forks would suddenly start failing their CI tests simultaneously, and none of them would know why.

51:05.460 --> 51:08.240
They would waste days assuming their own code broke the build.

51:08.540 --> 51:16.940
Exactly. You need the substrate, the infrastructure, to be perfectly stable so the developers can test their code variables against a known constant.

51:17.100 --> 51:18.820
So the environment is pinned.

51:19.260 --> 51:21.420
But how do they share it without stepping on each other?

51:21.740 --> 51:24.560
They use an orchestration pattern called the ephemeral pin.

51:24.740 --> 51:28.520
This sounds like a complex juggling act. Walk me through the life cycle of a pin.

51:28.920 --> 51:33.360
Let's say a developer working on the search service makes a code change and pushes a commit.

51:34.040 --> 51:36.600
Their GitHub Action CI pipeline triggers.

51:36.600 --> 51:42.580
First, it builds a new temporary container image containing their specific code change.

51:42.860 --> 51:49.740
Then the pipeline reaches out across the internet to the shared environment and edits that osdo image lock config map we just discussed.

51:50.060 --> 51:55.460
It replaces the canonical search image digest with the new untested image digest.

51:55.560 --> 51:57.540
It effectively borrows the slot in the lock file.

51:57.540 --> 52:02.620
Yes. And it annotates the lock file with metadata that says, I'm an ephemeral pin.

52:02.760 --> 52:05.620
I belong to this specific GitHub workflow run ID.

52:05.860 --> 52:08.660
And here's the canonical image I temporarily replaced.

52:08.660 --> 52:19.620
And because Flux is constantly watching that lock file for changes, it immediately detects the new digest and executes a rolling update to deploy the developer's new pod into the live cluster.

52:19.800 --> 52:20.240
Exactly.

52:20.240 --> 52:27.480
The pipeline then runs its battery of integration tests against the live API endpoints, validating the new code.

52:27.800 --> 52:33.480
And when the test suite finishes, whether it passes or fails, the pipeline executes a teardown script.

52:34.200 --> 52:39.400
It edits the lock file again, removing the ephemeral pin and putting the canonical image digest back.

52:40.100 --> 52:44.340
Flux sees the change, terminates the test pod, and restores the stable environment.

52:44.420 --> 52:46.460
It's an elegant borrow and return system.

52:46.460 --> 52:50.280
But in a fast-moving engineering organization, concurrency is inevitable.

52:51.040 --> 52:56.660
What happens if two developers, working on different branches of the search service, submit pull requests at the exact same time?

52:57.100 --> 52:59.020
Don't their pipelines fight over the lock file?

52:59.360 --> 53:02.880
The system enforces strict fail-closed gates to handle concurrency.

53:03.380 --> 53:05.960
The lock file operates on a last-write-wins basis.

53:06.320 --> 53:11.900
If pipeline A writes its pin and pipeline B overwrites it two seconds later, pipeline B owns the slot.

53:12.020 --> 53:13.420
So pipeline A just fails.

53:13.800 --> 53:14.400
Not blindly.

53:14.400 --> 53:16.420
Pipeline A does not blindly proceed.

53:16.780 --> 53:18.700
The system implements readiness as an API.

53:19.320 --> 53:23.140
Pipeline A queries a command like spy-set as JSON to verify its pod is ready.

53:23.680 --> 53:26.260
As part of that verification, it checks the lock file annotations.

53:26.680 --> 53:28.940
It sees that pipeline B has overwritten its pin.

53:29.280 --> 53:29.740
And then what?

53:30.140 --> 53:39.140
Pipeline A gracefully aborts, failing the CI run with a clear message telling the developer they were superseded by a newer run and they should retry.

53:39.360 --> 53:41.440
It doesn't blindly test the wrong code.

53:41.540 --> 53:42.700
It verifies ownership.

53:42.700 --> 53:44.100
Okay, concurrency is handled.

53:44.380 --> 53:46.120
But what about catastrophic failures?

53:47.120 --> 53:49.680
What if a test completely crashes midway through?

53:50.300 --> 53:58.340
What if the GitHub actions runner experiences an outage and the CI pipeline dies before it can execute the teardown script to put the canonical image back?

53:58.340 --> 54:05.580
Doesn't the broken test image just sit there forever, returning 500 errors and ruining the shared environment for the other seven repositories?

54:05.580 --> 54:06.900
They anticipated that.

54:07.040 --> 54:11.200
They built a scheduled sweeper cron job that runs continuously in the cluster.

54:11.620 --> 54:13.960
This routine periodically inspects the lock file.

54:14.080 --> 54:15.740
It looks at the metadata annotations.

54:15.900 --> 54:16.420
Ah, I see.

54:16.420 --> 54:32.240
If it finds an ephemeral pin that belongs to a GitHub action run that the GitHub API reports has already finished or died, or if it finds a pin that has been sitting there far longer than any reasonable integration test should take, the sweeper assumes the pipeline crashed.

54:32.700 --> 54:36.780
It automatically deletes the orphan pin and restores the canonical image.

54:36.780 --> 54:38.400
It's a self-cleaning battlefield.

54:39.020 --> 54:40.300
The environment heals itself.

54:41.020 --> 54:44.700
But there is one final glaring security question here.

54:44.780 --> 54:45.240
What's that?

54:45.460 --> 54:55.960
This entire orchestration requires the eight different code repositories to have the administrative permission to reach into a live Kubernetes cluster and edit a core config map.

54:56.680 --> 54:58.480
How do you manage that trust boundary?

54:58.980 --> 55:03.600
You can't just hand out cluster admin passwords to dozens of developers across eight repos.

55:03.600 --> 55:04.580
Absolutely not.

55:04.740 --> 55:07.640
Handing out static credentials would be a massive security breach.

55:08.020 --> 55:14.220
Instead, they rely on a brilliant application of GitHub's protected environments combined with Azure federated credentials.

55:14.360 --> 55:15.820
Oh, like we talked about with the database.

55:16.100 --> 55:16.540
Exactly.

55:17.120 --> 55:19.880
An outside repository is not trusted by default.

55:20.340 --> 55:24.540
To establish trust, the SPI stack operator runs an onboard command.

55:24.540 --> 55:35.300
This command creates a federated credential specifically tied to the OIDC issuer of the fork's GitHub repository and specifically bound to a protected environment within that repository.

55:35.880 --> 55:39.380
So the trust isn't a static password saved in a variable.

55:39.780 --> 55:45.600
It's cryptographically tied to the institutional identity of the GitHub Actions workflow itself.

55:45.760 --> 55:46.020
Yes.

55:46.020 --> 55:53.740
And the permissions granted to that workflow are microscopically narrow using strict role-based access control, or RBAC.

55:54.160 --> 56:04.040
The CI pipeline is granted a role that allows it to read the workload state to check readiness, and exactly one role that allows it to patch that specific OSDU image lock config map.

56:04.260 --> 56:06.140
That is the absolute limit of its power.

56:06.360 --> 56:07.920
It cannot create new Kubernetes resources.

56:08.100 --> 56:09.120
It cannot delete deployments.

56:09.220 --> 56:10.540
It cannot read Kubernetes secrets.

56:10.660 --> 56:11.220
None of it.

56:11.220 --> 56:16.020
Even if a malicious actor somehow compromised one of the CI pipelines, they couldn't take over the cluster.

56:16.400 --> 56:22.380
The worst they could do is temporarily change an image digest in a lock file, which the sweeper cron job would eventually revert anyway.

56:22.620 --> 56:26.720
It really is a master class in pragmatic, battle-tested engineering.

56:27.060 --> 56:30.100
When you step back and look at the macro architecture we've unpacked today,

